Below are separate Sender (Client) and Receiver (Server) C programs for all three IPC methods. These are simple OS lab practical codes.

### 1\. IPC using Pipes

Pipes work between related processes, so the sender creates a child process using `fork()`.

Sender.c

```c
#include<stdio.h>
#include<unistd.h>
#include<string.h>

int main()
{
    int fd[2];
    char msg[100]="Hello from Sender";

    pipe(fd);

    if(fork()==0)
    {
        close(fd[0]);
        write(fd[1],msg,strlen(msg)+1);
    }
    else
    {
        close(fd[1]);
        read(fd[0],msg,sizeof(msg));
        printf("Receiver: %s",msg);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Receiver.c

A normal unnamed pipe cannot be independently accessed by two separately started programs. For separate sender and receiver programs, use a named pipe (FIFO).

### 2\. IPC using Message Passing

Receiver.c (Server)

```c
#include<stdio.h>
#include<sys/ipc.h>
#include<sys/msg.h>

struct msg
{
    long type;
    char text[100];
};

int main()
{
    int id;
    struct msg m;

    id=msgget(1234,0666|IPC_CREAT);

    msgrcv(id,&m,sizeof(m.text),1,0);

    printf("Received: %s\n",m.text);

    msgctl(id,IPC_RMID,NULL);
    return 0;
}
```

Sender.c (Client)

```c
#include<stdio.h>
#include<sys/ipc.h>
#include<sys/msg.h>
#include<string.h>

struct msg
{
    long type;
    char text[100];
};

int main()
{
    int id;
    struct msg m;

    id=msgget(1234,0666|IPC_CREAT);

    m.type=1;
    strcpy(m.text,"Hello from Sender");

    msgsnd(id,&m,sizeof(m.text),0);

    printf("Message sent\n");
    return 0;
}
```

Execution:

```bash
gcc receiver.c -o receiver
gcc sender.c -o sender
```

First terminal:

```bash
./receiver
```

Second terminal:

```bash
./sender
```

### 3\. IPC using Shared Memory

Receiver.c (Server)

```c
#include<stdio.h>
#include<sys/ipc.h>
#include<sys/shm.h>

int main()
{
    int id;
    char *p;

    id=shmget(1234,1024,0666|IPC_CREAT);
    p=(char*)shmat(id,NULL,0);

    printf("Received: %s\n",p);

    shmdt(p);
    shmctl(id,IPC_RMID,NULL);

    return 0;
}
```

Sender.c (Client)

```c
#include<stdio.h>
#include<sys/ipc.h>
#include<sys/shm.h>
#include<string.h>

int main()
{
    int id;
    char *p;

    id=shmget(1234,1024,0666|IPC_CREAT);
    p=(char*)shmat(id,NULL,0);

    strcpy(p,"Hello from Sender");

    printf("Message sent\n");

    shmdt(p);
    return 0;
}
```

Execution:

```bash
gcc receiver.c -o receiver
gcc sender.c -o sender
```

First terminal:

```bash
./receiver
```

Second terminal:

```bash
./sender
```

For shared memory, the receiver must wait until the sender writes the message; the simple code above needs synchronization for reliable execution.

### Quick revision for practical exam

| IPC method      | Sender          | Receiver       |
| --------------- | --------------- | -------------- |
| Pipes           | write()         | read()         |
| Message Passing | msgsnd()        | msgrcv()       |
| Shared Memory   | shmat() + write | shmat() + read |

Important: For separate sender and receiver programs, use FIFO (`mkfifo`) for pipes. Unnamed pipes are normally used between related processes.
